<comment datatype="string">The &lt;a href="/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-80668"&gt;ald2&lt;/a&gt; gene of &lt;a href="/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1085"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Rhodospirillum rubrum&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; is located in a cluster with the &lt;a href="/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15512"&gt;mtnA&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15516"&gt;mtnP&lt;/a&gt; genes.
&lt;p&gt;
Anaerobic growth of the organism on &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=5-METHYLTHIOADENOSINE"&gt;&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-methyl-5'-thioadenosine&lt;/a&gt; results in a significant production of &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ETHYLENE-CMPD"&gt;ethene&lt;/a&gt;. However,  &amp;Delta;&lt;a href="/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15516"&gt;mtnP&lt;/a&gt;, &amp;Delta;&lt;a href="/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-15512"&gt;mtnA&lt;/a&gt;, and &amp;Delta;&lt;a href="/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-80668"&gt;ald2&lt;/a&gt; strains produced only low levels of &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ETHYLENE-CMPD"&gt;ethene&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/29133429"&gt;North17&lt;/a&gt;]. 
&lt;p&gt;
To investigate the role of &lt;a href="/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-80668"&gt;ald2&lt;/a&gt; the gene was cloned with a His&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt; tag and expressed in &lt;a href="/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-562"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, and the recombinant protein was purified under anaerobic conditions. Incubation of the purified enzyme (anaerobically) with &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-1063"&gt;&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-methyl-5-thio-D-ribulose 1-phosphate&lt;/a&gt; resulted in production of &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-23221"&gt;(2-methylsulfanyl)acetaldehyde&lt;/a&gt;, indicating that the enzyme has the activity of &lt;a href="/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-4.1.2.62"&gt;EC 4.1.2.62, 5-deoxyribulose 1-phosphate aldolase&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/29133429"&gt;North17&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
When the &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; activity were studied using radioactive isotope labeling, labeled &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-23221"&gt;(2-methylsulfanyl)acetaldehyde&lt;/a&gt; was not observed, and instead &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-7677"&gt;(2-methylsulfanyl)ethanol&lt;/a&gt; was detected, indicating that the aldehyde product is reduced &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; to an alcohol. It is the latter that is converted to &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ETHYLENE-CMPD"&gt;ethene&lt;/a&gt;, the reason why the genes were studied in the first place [&lt;a href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/29133429"&gt;North17&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
Subsequent work using purified recombinant protein has shown that the enzyme is also active with &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-23219"&gt;5-deoxy-D-ribulose 1-phosphate&lt;/a&gt;, affording production of &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=DIHYDROXY-ACETONE-PHOSPHATE"&gt;glycerone phosphate&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACETALD"&gt;acetaldehyde&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/31950558"&gt;North20&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The enzyme requires a divalent metal cation. The highest activity was acheived with Ni&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; and Co&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; [&lt;a href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/31950558"&gt;North20&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
